Summary of Working History and Results Task interpretation - The question describes a football match in the early 2000s between two European top-division clubs with specific clues: - One club was founded in the 1870s and, as of 2001, had a manager who met his spouse as a youth player at the club (with an “age 20” detail). - The other club’s assistant manager was a former team captain in the 1970s. - Combined league wins for that season totaled 35. - The goal was to identify the clubs, confirm all clues, find the relevant fixtures, and determine the away team’s third substitute, documenting all evidence, sources, and ambiguities. Step-by-step plan and tool usage 1) Identify clubs and season matching all clues; verify with reliable sources; list fixtures. - Tool used: internet-capable agent to research clubs, staff, season wins, and fixtures. - Hypothesis formed: Bolton Wanderers (founded 1874) with manager Sam Allardyce in 2001 (spouse met at Bolton as youth player) and Arsenal with assistant manager Pat Rice (former captain in late 1970s). The 2001–02 Premier League has combined wins of 35 (Arsenal 26 + Bolton 9). - Results returned: • Bolton Wanderers founded 1874; Sam Allardyce managed Bolton 1999–2007. Quote: “He met his wife Lynne Ward while still a youth team player at Bolton Wanderers, and the pair married on 1 June 1974.” Source: Wikipedia: Sam Allardyce. The “age 20” detail could not be confirmed and conflicts with his youth years (1969–1971). • Arsenal assistant manager Pat Rice: assistant 1996–2012; captain from 1977 and lifted the FA Cup in 1979. Sources: Wikipedia: Pat Rice; archived Arsenal profile. • 2001–02 Premier League wins: Arsenal W 26, Bolton W 9; combined 35. Source: 2001–02 FA Premier League (Wikipedia). • Fixtures: Arsenal v Bolton (22 Sep 2001, Highbury, 1–1) and Bolton v Arsenal (29 Apr 2002, Reebok, 0–2). Sources: 11v11 season page and match pages. - Key URLs: • Bolton Wanderers founding: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolton_Wanderers_F.C. • Sam Allardyce bio and spouse quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sam_Allardyce • Pat Rice bio: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pat_Rice • Pat Rice archived profile: https://web.archive.org/web/20170522141556/http://www.arsenal.com/history/profiles/565/pat-rice • 2001–02 Premier League table and stadiums: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2001%E2%80%9302_FA_Premier_League • Arsenal 2001–02 season context: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2001%E2%80%9302_Arsenal_F.C._season • 11v11 Arsenal matches (season page): https://www.11v11.com/teams/arsenal/tab/matches/season/2002/ • Match pages: Arsenal v Bolton (22 Sep 2001): https://www.11v11.com/matches/arsenal-v-bolton-wanderers-22-september-2001-8489/; Bolton v Arsenal (29 Apr 2002): https://www.11v11.com/matches/bolton-wanderers-v-arsenal-29-april-2002-12264/ 2) Retrieve detailed match reports with substitution minutes and order for both fixtures; determine the away team’s third substitute; cross-verify. - Tool used: internet-capable agent to collect match reports from multiple reputable sources (11v11, Transfermarkt, etc.). - Results returned: • Match 1 (Arsenal v Bolton, 22 Sep 2001, Highbury; away: Bolton): - 11v11 provides substitution minutes and on/off pairs: “Substitutions: Michael Ricketts for Dean Holdsworth 61; Djibril Diawara for Paul Warhurst 79; Rodney Wallace for Henrik Pedersen 80” URL: https://www.11v11.com/matches/arsenal-v-bolton-wanderers-22-september-2001-8489/ - Transfermarkt corroborates the on/off pairs but lists minutes as “Not reported”: Michael Ricketts ↔ Dean Holdsworth; Djibril Diawara ↔ Paul Warhurst; Rod Wallace ↔ Henrik Pedersen URL: https://www.transfermarkt.com/spielbericht/index/spielbericht/1000808 - Away-team substitution sequence (by minute): 61' Ricketts→Holdsworth; 79' Diawara→Warhurst; 80' Wallace→Pedersen. - Away team’s third substitute: Rodney Wallace (commonly reported as “Rodney Wallace” on 11v11; “Rod Wallace” on Transfermarkt), entered at 80' for Henrik Pedersen. • Match 2 (Bolton v Arsenal, 29 Apr 2002, Reebok; away: Arsenal): - 11v11 provides substitution minutes and on/off pairs: “Substitutions: Lee Dixon for Edu 67; Nwankwo Kanu for Dennis Bergkamp 70; Sol Campbell for Sylvain Wiltord 90” URL: https://www.11v11.com/matches/bolton-wanderers-v-arsenal-29-april-2002-12264/ - Transfermarkt corroborates on/off pairs but minutes “Not reported”: Lee Dixon ↔ Edu; Nwankwo Kanu ↔ Dennis Bergkamp; Sol Campbell ↔ Sylvain Wiltord URL: https://www.transfermarkt.com/spielbericht/index/spielbericht/1023375 - Away-team substitution sequence (by minute): 67' Dixon→Edu; 70' Kanu→Bergkamp; 90' Campbell→Wiltord. - Away team’s third substitute: Sol Campbell, entered at 90' for Sylvain Wiltord. - Note: Attempts to find second sources with explicit minutes (BBC, Soccerbase, worldfootball.net, Premier League site, Guardian, ESPN, FBref, Soccerway) were unsuccessful in this environment. However, no discrepancies appeared between 11v11 and Transfermarkt regarding identities and on/off pairs, and no same‑minute events occurred, so the third-sub determination is unambiguous. Evidence for clue verification - Club founded in the 1870s: Bolton Wanderers founded in 1874 (Wikipedia: Bolton Wanderers F.C.). - Manager as of 2001 with spouse anecdote: Sam Allardyce managed Bolton 1999–2007; “He met his wife Lynne Ward while still a youth team player at Bolton Wanderers...” (Wikipedia: Sam Allardyce). The “age 20” detail could not be verified and conflicts with documented youth years (1969–1971). - Assistant manager who was a former 1970s captain: Pat Rice served as Arsenal assistant manager (1996–2012); became club captain in 1977 and captained Arsenal in the late 1970s (Wikipedia; archived Arsenal profile). - Combined league wins totaling 35: 2001–02 Premier League final table shows Arsenal W 26, Bolton W 9; combined 35 (Wikipedia: 2001–02 FA Premier League). - Fixtures within early 2000s: 22 Sep 2001 (Arsenal v Bolton, 1–1) and 29 Apr 2002 (Bolton v Arsenal, 0–2) — both within the early 2000s (11v11 match pages). Uncertainties, conflicts, and alternative interpretations - The “age 20” part of the spouse anecdote is unverified and inconsistent with Sam Allardyce’s youth timeline. Nevertheless, the confirmed fact that he met his spouse while a youth player at Bolton fits the intent of the clue. - The question does not specify which of the two league fixtures is “the game.” Both matches in the 2001–02 season meet all described conditions. Therefore, the away team’s third substitute could be either of the following, depending on which match is intended: - Arsenal v Bolton (22 Sep 2001): Bolton away — third sub Rodney Wallace. - Bolton v Arsenal (29 Apr 2002): Arsenal away — third sub Sol Campbell. Final Answer \boxed{Rodney Wallace, Sol Campbell}
